window.__labLoaded({"id":"5.2","title":"The t-Distribution and Intervals for a Mean","goal":"Build a t-interval for a mean from nine nitrate readings, and see how the t critical value compares with z for small and larger samples.","question":"An environmental officer measured nitrate in nine river-water samples (mg/L): 3.1, 4.2, 2.8, 3.9, 4.5, 3.3, 4.1, 3.7, 3.5. We do not know the river's true SD, only the sample's. What range is a believable value for the river's true average nitrate level?","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The sample","text":"The lesson's nine nitrate readings (mg/L). Because sigma is unknown we use the sample SD s, and the standard error is s / sqrt(n).","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nnitrate = np.array([3.1, 4.2, 2.8, 3.9, 4.5, 3.3, 4.1, 3.7, 3.5])\nn = len(nitrate)\nmean = nitrate.mean()\nse = nitrate.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n)\n\nprint(f'mean = {mean:.3f}, s = {nitrate.std(ddof=1):.3f}, SE = {se:.3f}')","explain":["Load NumPy for arrays and square roots.","Load SciPy's stats tools, which include the t-distribution.","Store the nine readings in an array.","Count them: n = 9.","Compute the sample mean, our best single estimate.","Standard error = sample SD / sqrt(n); we use the sample SD s because sigma is unknown.","Print the mean, s and SE together."],"meaning":"The mean is 3.678 mg/L, the sample SD s is 0.554 and the standard error is 0.185. Because sigma is unknown we rely on s, which is itself an uncertain estimate, so we use the t-distribution instead of the normal.","out":"mean = 3.678, s = 0.554, SE = 0.185","figs":[]},{"title":"The t-interval in one line","text":"stats.t.interval takes the confidence level, the degrees of freedom (n minus 1), the centre and the standard error, and returns the lower and upper limits.","code":"low, high = stats.t.interval(0.95, df=n - 1, loc=mean, scale=se)\n\nprint(f'df = {n - 1}')\nprint(f'95% CI (t) = ({low:.2f}, {high:.2f}) mg/L')","explain":["stats.t.interval builds the 95% interval from the confidence level, df = n - 1, the centre (loc) and the standard error (scale).","Print the degrees of freedom, n - 1 = 8.","Print the 95% interval in mg/L."],"meaning":"With 8 degrees of freedom, the 95% t-interval is 3.25 to 4.10 mg/L. We are 95% confident the river's true average nitrate lies in that range.","out":"df = 8\n95% CI (t) = (3.25, 4.10) mg/L","figs":[]},{"title":"Check it by hand, and compare with z","text":"The interval is mean plus or minus t* times SE. Using z* = 1.96 by mistake gives a narrower interval that claims too much certainty.","code":"t_star = stats.t.ppf(0.975, df=n - 1)\nz_star = stats.norm.ppf(0.975)\n\nprint(f't* = {t_star:.3f}, margin = {t_star * se:.3f}')\nprint(f'by hand: ({mean - t_star * se:.2f}, {mean + t_star * se:.2f})')\nprint(f'with z*: ({mean - z_star * se:.2f}, {mean + z_star * se:.2f})  too narrow')","explain":["Find t*, the multiplier that captures the middle 95% when df = 8.","Find z*, the multiplier we would use if sigma were known.","Print t* and the margin of error (t* times SE).","Rebuild the interval by hand as mean minus and plus the margin; it should match the function.","Show the interval with z* instead; it is too narrow because it ignores the extra uncertainty from estimating s."],"meaning":"t* is 2.306 and the margin is 0.426, giving (3.25, 4.10) by hand, the same as the function. Using z* = 1.96 wrongly gives the narrower (3.32, 4.04), which claims more certainty than nine readings can support.","out":"t* = 2.306, margin = 0.426\nby hand: (3.25, 4.10)\nwith z*: (3.32, 4.04)  too narrow","figs":[]},{"title":"t versus z for different sample sizes","text":"The t critical value is larger for small samples, because s is a shaky estimate of sigma. As n grows, t* shrinks towards z* = 1.960.","code":"print('  n   df   t*      z*')\nfor k in (5, 10, 30):\n    print(f'{k:3d}  {k - 1:3d}  {stats.t.ppf(0.975, k - 1):.3f}  {stats.norm.ppf(0.975):.3f}')","explain":["Print column headings.","Loop over three sample sizes.","Print n, df, t* for that df and z* for comparison."],"meaning":"t* is 2.776 with 5 readings, 2.262 with 10 and 2.045 with 30, always above z* = 1.960. The smaller the sample, the more we must widen the interval. As n grows, t* shrinks toward z*.","out":"  n   df   t*      z*\n  5    4  2.776  1.960\n 10    9  2.262  1.960\n 30   29  2.045  1.960","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"nitrate <- c(3.1, 4.2, 2.8, 3.9, 4.5, 3.3, 4.1, 3.7, 3.5)\nt.test(nitrate)$conf.int\nqt(0.975, df = 8)\nqt(0.975, df = c(4, 9, 29))\nqnorm(0.975)","expect":"conf.int 3.252 to 4.104; qt df = 8: 2.306; qt for df 4, 9, 29: 2.776 2.262 2.045; qnorm 1.960","note":"t.test(x) tests against 0 by default, but we only use its conf.int, which is the 95% t-interval for the mean.","explain":["Enter the nine readings.","t.test(nitrate)$conf.int pulls out the 95% t-interval for the mean.","Critical value t* for df = 8.","t* for df 4, 9 and 29 (samples of 5, 10 and 30).","The z* critical value for comparison."],"read":"The interval prints 3.252 and 4.104 (the attr line shows 0.95). qt gives 2.306, then 2.776, 2.262 and 2.045 for the three sample sizes, and qnorm gives 1.960. These match Python."},"spss":{"menu":"Analyze > Descriptive Statistics > Explore (Statistics button: Descriptives, 95% confidence interval for the mean); critical values from Transform > Compute Variable with IDF.T","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / nitrate.\nBEGIN DATA\n3.1 4.2 2.8 3.9 4.5 3.3 4.1 3.7 3.5\nEND DATA.\nEXAMINE VARIABLES=nitrate\n  /PLOT=NONE\n  /STATISTICS=DESCRIPTIVES\n  /CINTERVAL 95.\nCOMPUTE t5 = IDF.T(0.975, 4).\nCOMPUTE t10 = IDF.T(0.975, 9).\nCOMPUTE t30 = IDF.T(0.975, 29).\nCOMPUTE z = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nLIST VARIABLES=t5 t10 t30 z /CASES=FROM 1 TO 1.","note":"Explore's Descriptives table shows the 95% Confidence Interval for Mean (3.25 to 4.10). IDF.T(0.975, df) gives the t critical value.","explain":["Declare one variable, nitrate.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","The nine readings.","Marks the end of the data.","EXAMINE gives descriptive statistics.","Turn off plots.","Ask for the descriptives table.","Set the confidence level to 95%.","t* for df = 4 (a sample of 5).","t* for df = 9.","t* for df = 29.","z*, for comparison.","List the four critical values."],"read":"In the Descriptives table, find 95% Confidence Interval for Mean: lower bound 3.25, upper bound 4.10. The LIST output shows 2.776, 2.262, 2.045 and 1.960, the critical values t and z."},"stata":{"code":"clear\ninput nitrate\n3.1\n4.2\n2.8\n3.9\n4.5\n3.3\n4.1\n3.7\n3.5\nend\nci means nitrate\ndisplay invttail(4, 0.025) \" \" invttail(9, 0.025) \" \" invttail(29, 0.025) \" \" invnormal(0.975)","note":"ci means gives the 95% t-interval. invttail(df, 0.025) is the upper-tail t critical value, which equals Python's stats.t.ppf(0.975, df).","explain":["Clear any data in memory.","Start entering data for one variable called nitrate.","Data row 1 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 2 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 3 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 4 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 5 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 6 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 7 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 8 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Data row 9 of 9: a nitrate reading, typed on its own line so Stata reads it as a new case.","Stop entering data.","ci means gives the mean, its standard error and the 95% t-interval.","Print the t critical values for df = 4, 9, 29 and then z* (invttail gives the upper-tail value)."],"read":"The ci means table shows Mean 3.678, Std. err. .185 and the 95% interval 3.252 to 4.104. The display line prints 2.776, 2.262, 2.045 and 1.960."},"conclusion":"With nine readings, the average nitrate level is 3.678 mg/L and the 95% t-interval is 3.25 to 4.10 mg/L. The t* of 2.306 is bigger than z* = 1.960 because estimating s from only nine values adds uncertainty. Caution: the t-interval assumes the readings are a random sample from a roughly bell-shaped population, which is hard to check with nine values.","practice":{"scenario":"A gardener measures the height (cm) of 12 seedlings grown with a new compost. The seed packet claims the average height is 30 cm. She wants a 95% and a 99% interval for the true average and to see whether 30 is a believable value.","data":"[31.2, 28.5, 33.0, 30.1, 27.8, 32.4, 29.6, 34.1, 30.8, 28.9, 31.7, 29.3]","tasks":["Find the mean, SD and standard error.","Build the 95% t-interval.","Build the 99% t-interval.","Is 30 cm inside each interval?"],"hint":"Use stats.t.interval(level, df=n-1, loc=mean, scale=se) with se = s / sqrt(n).","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nheight = np.array([31.2, 28.5, 33.0, 30.1, 27.8, 32.4, 29.6, 34.1, 30.8, 28.9, 31.7, 29.3])\nn = len(height)\nmean, se = height.mean(), height.std(ddof=1) / np.sqrt(n)\nprint(f'mean = {mean:.2f}, s = {height.std(ddof=1):.2f}, SE = {se:.3f}')\nfor level in (0.95, 0.99):\n    low, high = stats.t.interval(level, df=n - 1, loc=mean, scale=se)\n    print(f'{level:.0%} CI: ({low:.2f}, {high:.2f})')\nprint(f't* (95%, df={n - 1}) = {stats.t.ppf(0.975, n - 1):.3f}')","answer":"The mean is 30.62 cm, s = 1.93 and the SE is 0.557. The 95% interval is 29.39 to 31.84 and the 99% interval is wider, 28.89 to 32.35, using t* = 2.201 at 11 df for 95%. The claimed 30 cm is inside both, so these 12 seedlings give no reason to doubt the packet's claim.","out":"mean = 30.62, s = 1.93, SE = 0.557\n95% CI: (29.39, 31.84)\n99% CI: (28.89, 32.35)\nt* (95%, df=11) = 2.201","figs":[]}}});
